    "reason": "The issue specifies two mutually contradictory requirements when processing multiple chapter files that contain common headings such as `## Examples` and `## See also`:\n\n1. \"The page must not contain the same `id` twice.\"\n2. \"Each chapter's part of the output must stay byte-for-byte what `python -m mdlite chapter.md` prints for that chapter on its own today, because our tests compare every chapter against a stored snapshot of exactly that output.\"\n\nWhen rendered individually, each chapter file independently assigns the base slug (e.g. `id=\"examples\"`) to its first occurrence of a heading. If each chapter's section in the combined multi-file output remains byte-for-byte identical to its standalone output, the combined output will inevitably contain duplicate `id` attributes across chapters, violating Requirement 1. Conversely, if heading ids are deduplicated across files (e.g., suffixed with `-1`) or prefixed to prevent duplicate ids, subsequent chapters will no longer match their standalone byte-for-byte snapshot, violating Requirement 2.\n\nBecause these requirements are mutually exclusive and resolving them requires an architectural or product decision on how to handle cross-file id collisions, the issue is not actionable.",
